            <ArticleTitle>Invasive pancreatic cancer presenting as gastrointestinal hemorrhage--a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>390-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage is not uncommonly seen by the surgical practitioner. We present a case of a patient who presented with melena and syncope, who was subsequently found to have invasive metastatic pancreatic cancer as his source of bleed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
